The replay's Stagger window starting at its break (2026-11-27, the hold-at-a-break round; dropped)

replayrecord.ts derives a window's start as its end less its length; with engine assumption break-hold the break's
own hold moves the end at once, so the recorded start is the break + 14 frames. This clamps it to the sample that
first sees it (the break's frame). Measured as a leave-one-out variant on the break-hold branch (measure --base
f96757f, variant WINDOW_AT_BREAK=false, scratch switch): without it, against the branch with it, development 2 better,
3 worse, 2 mixed (plain 84.2% either way), earlier pending unchanged, hold-outs 2 better (plain 82.4 -> 82.5%,
mismatch 5.4 -> 5.3%: plain +8, mismatch -5). Dropped on the hold-outs: the start feeds the hidden holds' still spans
(buffreplay.ts staggerStill), and the late start lets a hidden hold rise in the hold's 14 frames, where the logs put
the break's hold at 0.54 s (16 frames) at the median, more than the engine's 14. The recorded start (views'
STAGGER lines, BuffReplay.staggers) is then 14 frames after the break. Would work once the still span is taken
from the break's own end (buffreplay.ts) or the hold's length is settled.

